# Feature Selection Implemented! 🎯

Great catch on noticing the AI was being trained on irrelevant columns like `Sl. No.`. In the real world, training a model on IDs, Names, or Dates will ruin its accuracy and introduce massive bias. I have now built a robust **Feature Selection** pipeline to solve this!

## What was built:

### 1. Database Schema Update
- I updated your Postgres database schema to include a `selectedFeatures` field inside the `Experiment` table. 
- This ensures we have a permanent audit trail of exactly which columns the AI was trained on for compliance.

### 2. The Experiment Wizard Update
- Go back to the **Projects** page and start a new experiment.
- You will notice a brand new **Step 4: Select Features** checklist!
- Once you pick your target column, it automatically loads all other columns. You can now uncheck the blank `Sl. No.` column (now labeled `Unnamed Feature 1`) so the model ignores it completely!

### 3. Python ML Worker (Data Filtering)
- The Python backend now reads your `selectedFeatures` from Redis.
- Before it trains the `RandomForestRegressor`, it runs `X = X[selected_features]`. This drops all unselected columns from the Pandas DataFrame, ensuring the AI only learns from the exact data you chose.

### 4. Smart Deployment Forms
- Finally, when you deploy the model on the **Deployments** page, the "Magic Form" will **only** ask you for the columns you selected!
- Since you didn't train the model on the `Sl. No.` column, the Next.js frontend is smart enough to remove it from the testing UI.

> [!TIP]
> **Try it out!**
> 1. Go to the **Projects** page (`/experiments`).
> 2. Create a new experiment using your Dataset.
> 3. Select `Salary` as the target.
> 4. In **Step 4**, uncheck `Unnamed Feature 1` (which corresponds to your blank `Sl. No.` column).
> 5. Click Start Training.
> 6. Once it completes, go to **Deployments** and deploy it. The testing form will now only ask you for `YEARSEXPERIENCE`!
